   - 4 heads and 0 tails (H: 4, T: 0)
   - 3 heads and 1 tail (H: 3, T: 1)
   - 2 heads and 2 tails (H: 2, T: 2)

   We will calculate the number of ways to achieve each of these distributions using the binomial coefficient \( \binom{n}{k} \), which represents the number of ways to choose \( k \) successes (heads) in \( n \) trials (tosses).

### Step-by-Step Calculations
