For a prime \( p \), the exponent of \( p \) in \( n! \) can be calculated using the formula:
\[
e_p(n!) = \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \left\lfloor \frac{n}{p^k} \right\rfloor
\]
This formula counts how many multiples of \( p \), \( p^2 \), \( p^3 \), etc., are present in the numbers from 1 to \( n \).

Once we have the prime factorization of \( 10! \), we can determine how many integers \( x \) satisfy \( x^3 \) being a factor of \( 10! \). If \( 10! \) has the prime factorization:
\[
10! = p_1^{e_1} \cdot p_2^{e_2} \cdots p_k^{e_k}
\]
then for \( x^3 \) to be a factor of \( 10! \), we need:
\[
x = p_1^{a_1} \cdot p_2^{a_2} \cdots p_k^{a_k}
\]
where \( 3a_i \leq e_i \) for each prime \( p_i \). This implies:
\[
a_i \leq \left\lfloor \frac{e_i}{3} \right\rfloor
\]
The number of choices for \( a_i \) is \( \left\lfloor \frac{e_i}{3} \right\rfloor + 1 \) (including the option \( a_i = 0 \)).

